# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sub-grid drop -> Lagrangian particle transfer
#
# Detached non-core components wholly on the finest level can transfer at any distance
# from the core, when their equivalent-volume diameter is at most the configured maximum.
# Refill requires component donor cells with at least 50% gas; no donors means no removal.
#
# COUPLING returns momentum and total energy to the fluid. The mechanical part of src(4)
# is minus the hydrodynamic impulse dotted with the particle endpoint-average velocity;
# the thermal part is minus the accepted particle internal-energy change. Mechanical work
# includes resolved stress. Drag dissipation is monitored separately and is not added twice.
# Converted particles carry the removed mass and total energy (translation plus internal
# energy). Gunn particle heat exchange is enabled, using the liquid EOS and k_g=mu_g*cp_g/Pr.
# Gunn porosity is fixed at 1; Eulerian gas fraction still weights sampled gas properties.
# The existing midpoint push limits thermal substeps to half the local thermal response time.
# There is no evaporation/phase-change model. Particle mass and diameter stay fixed after birth.
# Gas sampling retains every positive usable gas fraction, normalized over the stencil.
# A gas-free stencil culls the last accepted particle state; separate loss ledgers
# book mass, momentum and total energy. No liquid-drag fallback or reabsorption.
#
# Additional source diffusion defaults to zero; trilinear deposition spreads the reaction
# over the coupling stencil. Diameter ratio alone does not establish dilute local loading.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
Drop transfer:              .true.
Drop transfer maximum diameter ratio: 1.0
# Minimum diameter / finest-grid spacing (formerly coupling-grid units).
# Eligible structures strictly below this limit are flotsam; zero disables this size cull.
Drop transfer diameter threshold: 0.1
Drop transfer nsteps:       10
# Core-box padding for wake tagging only; no transfer proximity restriction.
!Drop transfer margin:      0.5

# Spray solver
# Osnes et al. (2023) is the only drag law in amrlpt that is in range at the Re_p these drops
# see; Schiller-Naumann under-predicts drag by ~10x. Watch 'Re max'/'Ma max'/'Kn max' in
# monitor/spray to confirm the model is being used inside its intended range.
# Drag samples molecular gas viscosity and gas sound speed, not effective mixture
# viscosity/sound speed. Gas-property interpolation is normalized by gas fraction;
# pure-liquid cells have zero weight, and a gas-free particle stencil is an error.
# Levels below 'Max level' that particles are capped to. The cap coarsens BOTH fluid
# sampling and source deposition; the reaction is transferred to finer fluid levels.
# The maximum transfer diameter is 'Drop transfer maximum diameter ratio' times
# the minimum spacing at the particle level cap. The default ratio is 1.0.
# The initialization check rejects a transfer threshold that exceeds 'Particle maximum d/dx'.
# Watch 'd/dx couple' in monitor/spray to confirm the configured upper bound.
Particle level offset: 2
Particle maximum d/dx: 1.0

Particle drag model:  Osnes
Particle max CFL:     0.5
!Particle max dt:     1e-3
!Particle filter width: 0.5
Particle collisions:  .false.
!Particle collision time: 1e-2
!Spray output period: 1e-1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Wake coarsening (off by default; the settings below are a suggested starting point)
#
# Of the four tagging criteria only the SGS cell-Reynolds test is a turbulence-RESOLUTION test,
# and in a turbulent wake it never switches off - which is what keeps the wake refined far
# downstream. These knobs make that one criterion progressively harder to satisfy with level and
# with downstream distance, only inside the wake. With 'Wake tag density' enabled, the density
# Laplacian shares the ramp and cap. Liquid pressure and Ducros criteria are not wake-ramped.
# The density indicator is bounded by 1: a ramped threshold >= 1 disables that criterion.
#
#   Re_eff = Re_tag * (Wake tag ramp)^(lvl - Wake tag level)     for lvl above the level
#                   * 2^((x - x_start)/Wake tag length)          for x beyond x_start
#
# Both multipliers apply only for x > x_start; upstream the original thresholds are retained.
# Here x_start = max(Wake tag x0, downstream face of the padded core box).
# This protects the primary-drop region from wake coarsening. The box does not restrict
# transfer: eligible detached structures can transfer at any distance from the core.
#
# 'Wake max level offset' is a true relative cap on refinement requested by the SGS-Re criterion
# and, when 'Wake tag density' is true, by the density criterion inside the wake.  Other criteria
# remain free to refine shocks and Eulerian liquid interfaces to Max level.  With Max level=6 and
# offset=2, wake criteria can request grids through level 4 but cannot request levels 5 or 6.
# AMReX grid clustering/proper nesting can still extend a fine box a short distance into the wake,
# and independent shock/interface criteria are deliberately unaffected.
#
# Suggested: allow full turbulence refinement to level 2, then quadruple the threshold per level
# while also imposing the explicit two-level wake cap, and double the threshold again every
# 2 length units past x=1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
Wake tag level:  2
Wake tag ramp:   4.0
Wake max level offset: 2
Wake tag x0:     1.0
Wake tag length: 2.0
Wake tag density: .true.
